    "@type" : "Question",
    "name" : "Who is subject to Transfer Pricing regulations in India?",
    "acceptedAnswer" : {
      "@type" : "Answer",
      "text" : "<p>Transfer Pricing (TP) regulations apply to international transactions between associated enterprises, where at least one party is a non-resident. Certain specified domestic transactions between related Indian entities are also covered under Indian TP regulations. In certain circumstances, transactions with third parties may also fall under Indian TP regulations.</p>"
    }
  }, {
    "@type" : "Question",
    "name" : "Which compliances are required under Indian Transfer Pricing regulations?",
    "acceptedAnswer" : {
      "@type" : "Answer",
      "text" : "<p>Taxpayers must file Form 3CEB certified by a Chartered Accountant, and, if thresholds are met, maintain contemporaneous documentation including the Local File, Master File, and Country-by-Country Report.</p>"
    }
  }, {
    "@type" : "Question",
    "name" : "What are the penalties for non-compliance with Transfer Pricing regulations?",
    "acceptedAnswer" : {
      "@type" : "Answer",
      "text" : "<p>Penalties range from 2% to 6% of the transaction value for non-compliance, including failure to maintain documentation or report transaction, and for maintaining and furnishing incorrect details. An additional penalty of INR1,00,000 applies for not filing Form 3CEB. Further penalties may apply for under-reporting or misreporting income due to TP adjustments.</p>"
    }
  }, {
    "@type" : "Question",
    "name" : "What are the alternate dispute resolution mechanisms available to the taxpayers in India for Transfer Pricing disputes?",
    "acceptedAnswer" : {
      "@type" : "Answer",
      "text" : "<p>Indian TP regulations offer dispute resolution mechanisms such as Safe Harbour Rules, Advance Pricing Agreements (APAs), and Mutual Agreement Procedures (MAPs) to enhance certainty and prevent double taxation.</p>"
    }
  }, {
    "@type" : "Question",
    "name" : "How can businesses proactively manage Transfer Pricing risks in India?",
    "acceptedAnswer" : {
      "@type" : "Answer",
      "text" : "<p>Businesses can manage TP risks by maintaining strong documentation, conducting regular benchmarking, implementing consistent policies, automating processes through technology, and leveraging APAs, MAPs, or Safe Harbour options for certainty and dispute avoidance.</p>"
    }
  } ]
}
